{# THE GATE — the same three answers, in both containers.

   The File control is DEAD, visually and functionally, until a kind is chosen,
   one line says what happened and the place is marked. A quiet line names
   whatever is still missing rather than making anybody press a disabled button to
   find out.

   THE SERVER REFUSES THE SAME FILING for the same three reasons
   ({@see IncidentReportController::create}); this only says so sooner. The button
   ships disabled so a filer without JavaScript is never shown a control that
   would be refused, and the noscript block hands it back because without the
   controller nothing can enable it.

   THE MISSING LIST IS COMPUTED HERE TOO, not only in the controller: a filing
   that arrived from a record already has its place, and a gate that asked for it
   anyway until the first keystroke would be lying on first paint.

   CANCEL IS A REAL LINK, in both containers — the honest back link on the page,
   the explicit close on the drawer. Nothing here can be dismissed by clicking
   beside it.

   Parameters: controller, chosen, prefill, cancelUrl, compact
   ========================================================================== #}
{% set missing = [] %}
{% if not chosen %}{% set missing = missing|merge(['choose a category']) %}{% endif %}
{# A line the source record already wrote COUNTS AS FILLED: the filer said what
   happened once, in the field, and the form must not pretend they did not. #}
{% if not prefill.note %}{% set missing = missing|merge(['describe what happened']) %}{% endif %}
{% if prefill.latitude is null or prefill.longitude is null %}
    {% set missing = missing|merge(['mark where it happened']) %}
{% endif %}

{# THE QUICK-FILE PROMISE, IN ONE LINE, BESIDE THE CONTROL THAT ACTS ON IT.
   It was a whole headed section once — "People & evidence — can wait", four rows
   of prose each wearing a "can wait" pill, and an explainer under them. That
   section had NO INPUTS: a heading and a step number over nothing to fill in,
   which teaches a reader that a heading here does not mean a question.

   The promise was always the point, and a promise is a sentence. It is stated
   HERE rather than up in the form because this is where somebody decides whether
   they have done enough to file — and it is above the bar rather than inside it
   so it never competes with the gate's own list of what is still missing.

   Both containers, one line, one wording: the discipline cannot drift between
   the page and the drawer if there is only one of it. #}
<p class="ro-promise">Only the kind, what happened and where are needed now. Severity, people, evidence and money are
    added on the record afterwards.</p>

<div class="{{ compact ? 'ro-dfoot' : 'ro-filebar' }}">
    {# The gate REPLACES the reassuring line rather than crowding in beside it:
       both are the same quiet mono note in the same place, and only one of them
       is worth reading at a time. #}
    <span class="hint" {{ stimulus_target(controller, 'hint') }}{{ missing is empty ? '' : ' hidden' }}>Nothing is
        written until you file. A filed incident starts at <b class="acc">reported</b> and cannot skip
        verification.</span>
    <span class="ro-gate" {{ stimulus_target(controller, 'gate') }}{{ missing is empty ? ' hidden' : '' }}>{{ missing|join(' · ') }}</span>
    <span class="sp"></span>
    <a class="tgl" href="{{ cancelUrl }}">Cancel</a>
    <button class="cta ro-file" type="submit" {{ stimulus_target(controller, 'file') }}{{ missing is empty ? '' : ' disabled' }}>
        {{- compact ? 'File →' : 'File incident →' -}}
    </button>
</div>
<noscript>
    <div class="{{ compact ? 'ro-dfoot' : 'ro-filebar' }}">
        <span class="hint">Choose a kind above, say what happened and give the place — the report is refused without
            all three.</span>
        <span class="sp"></span>
        <button class="cta" type="submit">File incident →</button>
    </div>
</noscript>
